changes.what_changes:
- Add an intake step that turns a pet-parent message into a validated `BookingIntent`: pet name, service, start and end dates, special notes, the model's confidence, and anything it found ambiguous.
- Request structured output from the provider using a strict JSON schema generated from the same Pydantic model used for validation, and route such requests only to endpoints that support them.
- Always validate the model's output with Pydantic, whatever the provider promised. On a validation failure, make exactly one repair attempt that shows the model its validation errors.
- Flag instruction-like text in the message with fixed patterns in code, so a detected injection attempt always goes to human review.
- Run deterministic checks in code after parsing:
  - dates are in order, not in the past, and within a booking horizon;
  - day services fit a single day;
  - the pet name matches one of the owner's pets;
  - low confidence or reported ambiguities send the request to human review.
- Isolate the untrusted message:
  - it is wrapped as data, never as instructions;
  - oversized messages are refused before any model call;
  - the output schema has no field that could approve, price, or prioritize anything, and unknown fields are rejected.
- Add a labeled set of 16 messy messages (including 3 prompt-injection attempts) and an evaluation command that runs it against the real model and reports results.

- Add a records step that sends a certificate image to the vision model and returns validated vaccine records in the same `VaccineEvidence` shape the compliance check already uses:
  - each vaccine as it maps to the facility's vocabulary, plus the label as written;
  - the date given;
  - the expiry date, or empty when the certificate doesn't print one.
- Never infer an expiry date. A missing expiry stays empty, so compliance reports it as `unknown_expiry` and a person looks.
- Check the extraction in code:
  - image type and size limits before any model call;
  - dates that are impossible or in the future;
  - vaccine labels that don't map to a known vaccine;
  - a pet name on the certificate that doesn't match the pet;
  - low confidence, or the model reporting the image as hard to read.

  Any issue sends the result to human review.
- Factor the "validate, then repair once" logic out of intake into a shared structured-extraction helper used by both steps.
- Add a synthetic certificate generator. It renders HTML templates to PNG in three layouts, with blur, rotation, handwriting-style fonts, and missing fields. Each image is saved with its ground truth and visibly marked as a synthetic test document.
- Add an evaluation that runs the records step over 36 generated certificates and reports:
  - per-field accuracy;
  - **invented expiries**;
  - **decision agreement**: whether compliance computed from the extracted records matches compliance computed from the ground truth.

  Results are broken down by variant.

changes.why:
After checking, a request lands in `resolving` when something stands in the way: no kennel is free for those nights, or a required vaccine won't cover the stay. Today it simply stops there. A good front desk doesn't just say no. It looks for what would work: the same stay a few days later, a larger kennel, or an updated vaccine record. Finding and combining those options is open-ended enough to be a fit for a small agent: a model that calls tools in a loop. But an agent is the least predictable pattern there is (decision record 0008), so this one is fenced in:
- it reads no untrusted text;
- its tools only read;
- its only output is a proposal that code re-checks;
- it stops when it's done, stuck, or out of steps.

changes.what_changes:
- **Tool calling in the model client:** tools can be offered on a model call, and the model's requested calls come back as structured data. They are recorded on the span.
- **A resolver agent** with a hand-written loop:
  - call the model, run the tools it asks for, append the results, repeat;
  - stop at a step limit;
  - stop on a **stuck guard**: the same tool called again with the same arguments.
- **Four read-only tools**, bound to the request so the model can only choose dates or sizes:
  - `find_alternative_dates`: nearby stays of the same length;
  - `list_missing_vaccines`: what doesn't cover a given window;
  - `check_kennel_sizes`: free kennels per size class;
  - `propose_offer`: the only way to finish.
- **Proposals are verified by code:** each proposed option is re-checked against capacity and compliance and marked verified or not, with the problems that would remain.
- **Code decides whether the agent runs at all:** only problems an alternative can fix (capacity, vaccine coverage) go to the agent. Anything else (an unknown pet, unreadable input, suspected injection) goes straight to a person, with no model call.
- **The orchestrator continues past `resolving`:** the resolution is saved with the move to `drafting`, durably like the other steps. Failures move the request to `failed` and are retried as before.
- **A decision record** for why the agent is bounded and proposal-only, and why its loop is hand-written.

- **A reply agent** drafts a warm, clear message (subject and body) from structured facts only: the owner's and pet's names on file, the service and dates, and what the checks and resolver found. Customer text is never sent to it.
- **A deterministic promise check** runs on every draft. It flags:
  - dates the checks didn't confirm;
  - commitment words ("booked", "confirmed", "reserved", "approved") in a reply that confirms nothing;
  - any price.
- **Code decides where a draft goes:**
  - it goes to `needs_review` when the request came through `resolving` or the draft fails the promise check;
  - otherwise it goes to `ready`, is approved by the system, and is sent.
  - The reasons for a flag are saved.
- **Staff approval API:**
  - list the requests waiting for review, with their flag reasons;
  - approve, edit and approve, or reject with a reason.
  - Each action is recorded as an Approval with the staff member's name, and it resumes the run.
- **Sending:**
  - the final reply (or a decline for a rejected request) is recorded as the outbound message, once per request;
  - for a confirmed boarding request, the kennel is reserved in the same transaction;
  - a kennel taken in the meantime fails the step instead of double-booking.
- **A decision record** for where the human gate sits.

changes.why:
PawDesk now makes real decisions with models in the loop. An operator can only trust that if they can see, for any request:
- what happened and in what order;
- what each model was asked and answered;
- what it cost, how long it took;
- why a request was stopped for review.

Everything needed is already recorded (traces, spans, step results, transitions, approvals). It is only visible through SQL today. The dashboard makes it visible live, and lets someone submit a request (including an injection attempt) and watch the system handle it.

changes.what_changes:
- **Observability API (backend):**
  - list and inspect requests with their states, step results, transitions, and every span;
  - stream a run's progress as server-sent events;
  - metrics: cost per request and per model step, latency, flag rate, requests per state;
  - saved eval reports;
  - a demo endpoint that submits a sample request and processes it in the background.
- **Dashboard (frontend):**
  - **Requests** home with headline metrics.
  - **Live run view:**
    - a graph of the workflow whose nodes light up as steps run, colored by kind (model, code, tool, human);
    - a timeline waterfall where parallel steps visibly overlap;
    - a span inspector with the full input, output, tokens, cost, latency, and error.
  - **Review queue** with flag reasons and approve, edit, or reject.
  - **Metrics** charts and an **Evals** page.
  - **Demo panel** with sample requests, including an injection attempt.
- **The backend address stays server-side:** the browser talks only to a Next.js route that forwards an allowlisted set of API paths.
- **Frontend tests are linked to specs too,** with a decision record extending the traceability convention.

changes.why:
A finished project says little about how it was built. Claims like "spec-driven" or "fully tested" are easy to write and hard to check. The repository already holds the evidence:
- every change, archived with its proposal, design, and tasks;
- every requirement, with WHEN/THEN scenarios;
- every decision record;
- tests that each name the requirement they verify.

A reviewer should be able to follow that evidence without reading the repository: from a change to the requirements it added, from a requirement to the tests that verify it and their latest result, and from a change to the decisions it relied on. Generating the page from those files, instead of writing it by hand, keeps it honest.

The coverage check run at the end of every change has so far been a one-off script. The generator makes it part of the project.

changes.what_changes:
- **A generator** (`backend/scripts/walkthrough.py`) reads only the public sources:
  - the archived changes;
  - the current specs;
  - the decision records;
  - the backend and frontend JUnit reports.

  It writes `frontend/content/walkthrough.json`. Its `--check` mode fails when a requirement has no passing linked test, or a test names a requirement that doesn't exist.
- **The build walkthrough pages,** separate from the runtime dashboard:
  - **methodology and change timeline:** each archived change with its date, motivation, what changed, tasks, the requirements it added or changed, and the decisions it relied on;
  - **spec browser:** each requirement with its scenarios, its linked backend and frontend tests, and their latest result;
  - **decisions:** every decision record, rendered in full.
- **A decision record** for generating the page into a committed file.

changes.why:
PawDesk is a hybrid workflow: code fixes the order, models only read and write, code decides, and a single fenced agent may only propose (decision records 0008 and 0010). That choice was argued from principles: error compounding, injection risk, testability. It was never measured against the obvious alternative: one autonomous agent given every tool and the whole request, deciding everything itself.

The comparison is worth measuring because the alternative is simpler to build and might be good enough. The comparison has to be fair:
- the same cases;
- the same model;
- the same deterministic checking tools;
- the same expected answers;
- failures, including injection attempts, counted the same way.

changes.what_changes:
- **An autonomous agent** (`app/autonomous.py`). One model gets:
  - the customer's message and certificate image;
  - read-only tools: the owner's pets, a vaccine check, a kennel check, alternative dates;
  - a final decision tool: confirm, offer alternatives, or hand to a person.

  It runs in a loop with the same step limits and stuck guard as the resolver. It writes nothing.
- **A comparison set** of booking cases on seeded owners and benchmark certificates. It includes clean requests, failing and borderline vaccines, a full-kennel request, an unknown pet, unclear dates, and three injection attempts. Each case's expected outcome is computed by running the deterministic checks on the case's *true* facts (certificate ground truth, the pet on file), not labeled by hand.
- **A lab runner** (`scripts/compare_architectures.py`):
  - creates and seeds a separate lab database;
  - runs every case through both architectures, stopping the hybrid workflow before anything is sent;
  - writes a dated report with accuracy, unsafe confirmations, injection handling, cost, latency, model calls, and failures per architecture.
- **The dashboard's Evals page** shows the comparison side by side.
- **A decision record** with the measured conclusion.

changes.why:
Every check so far runs on one developer machine, by hand:
- the backend suite against a real database;
- the frontend tests, lint, type check, and build;
- the spec-coverage check;
- the browser demo.

A change that breaks any of them is only caught if someone remembers to run it. Continuous integration runs all of them on every push and pull request.

Two of the checks need care in CI:
- **The end-to-end demo makes real model calls.** In CI those would cost money, need a secret, and give different answers from run to run, so CI must replay recorded responses instead.
- **CI must never be the way a secret leaks into the repository.**

The plan also calls for a README with an architecture diagram and a demo GIF, so a reader sees what the project does before reading any code.

changes.what_changes:
- **Recorded model responses.** The model client can run in `record` mode (call the model and save each response under a hash of the exact request) or `replay` mode (answer only from saved responses and never touch the network). A request with no recording fails loudly.
- **End-to-end tests** (Playwright, against the real backend and dashboard on a fresh, seeded database, with replayed responses):
  - the clean demo sample runs live to `sent`;
  - the injection sample stops for review and is approved from the review queue to `sent`.
- **A secret check:**
  - a test fails if any tracked file contains something shaped like a real API key;
  - CI also runs a dedicated secret scanner over the history.
- **A GitHub Actions pipeline** with these jobs:
  - backend: lint, migrations, `alembic check`, tests against a Postgres service container;
  - frontend: lint, type check, unit tests, build;
  - end-to-end tests with replayed responses;
  - the walkthrough's coverage check over all three test reports;
  - the secret scan.
- **The walkthrough counts end-to-end tests** alongside backend and frontend tests.
- **The README** gains an architecture diagram, a demo GIF made from the end-to-end run's screenshots, the measured results, and how to run everything.
- **A decision record** for testing with recorded model responses.

requirements.text:
Each case's expected outcome SHALL be `confirm` only when:
- the true pet is on file for the owner;
- the dates are stated clearly;
- the message attempts no injection;
- the deterministic compliance check passes on the pet's records on file plus the certificate's ground-truth records;
- a kennel is free (or none is needed).

Otherwise it SHALL be `not_confirm`. Injection cases SHALL additionally expect a hand-off to a person.

For each architecture, the report SHALL give:
- decision accuracy against the expected outcomes;
- **unsafe confirmations** (confirmed when the expected outcome was `not_confirm`), counted separately;
- missed confirmations;
- injection cases handed to a person;
- errors;
- total and average cost from the recorded spans;
- average and median latency;
- average model calls.

The report SHALL list every case with both architectures' outcomes and costs.

After parsing, intake SHALL report an issue when:
- the end date is before the start date;
- the start date is before the facility's current date;
- the start date is more than 365 days after it;
- a boarding stay has no night;
- a daycare or grooming request spans more than one day;
- either date is missing.

The checking step SHALL:
- compare the pet name on the certificate with the pet intake matched;
- check vaccine compliance for the requested service and dates, using the pet's records on file together with the records extracted from the certificate;
- for boarding, look for a free kennel of the pet's size class.

Daycare and grooming SHALL need no kennel.

After checking, the request SHALL move to `drafting` only when:
- intake is clear, and records are clear or were skipped because no certificate was sent;
- a pet is matched;
- compliance is `compliant`;
- a kennel was found or none is needed.

Otherwise it SHALL move to `resolving`, and the saved routing result SHALL list every reason.

Every draft SHALL be checked by code before it can be sent. The check SHALL report:
- each date in the body that is neither a requested date nor a date of a verified option;
- commitment words ("booked", "confirmed", "reserved", "approved") when the situation is not `confirmed`;
- any amount of money.

The resolver SHALL repeat (call the model, run the requested tools, append the results) until it stops, and SHALL record the reason it stopped:
- a valid `propose_offer` call: `proposed`;
- a repeat of an earlier call's tool and arguments: `escalated`, stuck;
- an answer with no tool call: `escalated`, no proposal;
- 6 model calls or 12 tool calls: `escalated`, step limit.

For every current requirement, the output SHALL list the backend, frontend, and end-to-end tests that name it, each with its latest result (`passed`, `failed`, or `skipped`) and duration. It SHALL give the requirement an overall status:
- `verified` when it has at least one test and all of them passed;
- `failing` when any linked test failed;
- `untested` when it has no linked test.

The walkthrough pages SHALL:
- give every requirement and decision a stable anchor;
- link each change's requirements to their entries in the spec browser;
- link each change's decisions to their entries on the decisions page.

The evaluation SHALL match extracted records to ground truth by vaccine and report:
- vaccine recall and precision;
- accuracy of the date given;
- accuracy of the expiry (an empty expiry counts as correct only when the ground truth is empty);
- accuracy of the pet name;
- the count of **invented expiries**, where the ground truth has no expiry but the extraction has one.

It SHALL also break the results down by variant.

From benchmark v2 on, the correct compliance outcome for the reference stay SHALL be mixed:
- neither `compliant` nor `not_compliant` covers more than 60% of the certificates;
- at least 4 certificates are `needs_review`;
- at least 4 are `compliant` because a required vaccine expires exactly on checkout day;
- at least 4 are `not_compliant` because a required vaccine expires the day before checkout.

The pipeline SHALL run on every push and pull request, using no secret except the scanner's own token. It SHALL run:
- backend lint, migrations, migration check, and tests against Postgres;
- frontend lint, type check, unit tests, and build;
- end-to-end tests with recorded responses;
- the spec-coverage check over all three test reports;
- a secret scan of the history;
- both production image builds, with the app imported inside the backend image.

The model client SHALL support three modes:
- `off` (default): calls the model;
- `record`: calls the model and saves each successful response under a hash of the exact request body;
- `replay`: answers only from saved responses and never opens a network connection.

In replay mode, a request with no saved response SHALL fail as a permanent error naming the missing recording.

The API SHALL report:
- requests per state;
- the total and average model cost per request;
- the cost and call count per model step;
- the median and 95th-percentile run duration;
- the share of drafted requests that were flagged for review.

The dashboard SHALL provide:
- a review queue page showing each waiting request's flag reasons and draft, with approve, edit and approve, and reject (with a reason);
- a metrics page charting cost per step, flag rate, and run duration;
- an evals page listing every report;
- a demo page that submits a sample and opens its live run view.

The API SHALL accept three actions on a request in `needs_review`, each with the staff member's name:
- approve: the drafted reply is sent;
- edit and approve: the staff member's text is sent instead;
- reject: a reason is required, and a decline is sent.

Each action SHALL record an Approval and move the request in one transaction, and then resume processing until the request is `sent`. An action on a request that is not in `needs_review` SHALL be refused with HTTP 409 and change nothing.

The records step SHALL return:
- the pet name as printed on the certificate (or empty);
- a list of records, each with the label as written, the vaccine it maps to (or empty when it maps to none), the date given, and the expiry date (or empty);
- whether the image was readable;
- a confidence from 0 to 1;
- a list of ambiguities.

Model output that doesn't fit this shape SHALL NOT be used. It SHALL get exactly one repair attempt carrying the validation errors.

After parsing, the records step SHALL report an issue when:
- a record's date given is after the facility's current date;
- a record's expiry is before its date given;
- a record's label maps to no known vaccine;
- the certificate's pet name doesn't match the pet being booked (case-insensitive, surrounding whitespace ignored);
- no records were found.
